.\" Man page for kirby (main.c)
.TH KIRBY 1 "May 2026" "0.2.0" "Kirby Manual"
.SH NAME
kirby \- a language interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kirby
.RB [ -h ]
.RB [ -v ]
.RB [ -r ]
.RB [ -f \fIfile\fR ]
.RB [ -c \fIsource\fR ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kirby
is the command‑line driver for a Kirby interpreter written in C.  It
initialises the virtual machine, loads the standard library, and then
executes code according to the selected mode.

The program supports the following operation modes:

.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short usage summary and exit.

.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the interpreter version (the value of
.BR KIRBY_VERSION )
and exit.

.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-repl
Start an interactive Read‑Eval‑Print Loop (REPL) after loading the
standard library.  The REPL reads lines from the user via
.BR readline (3),
stores history, and evaluates each line using
.BR interpret (3).
Typing
.I exit
or sending EOF terminates the REPL.

.TP
.BR \-f " " \fIfile\fR ", " \-\-file=\fIfile\fR
Execute the Kirby source file
.I file
after the standard library has been loaded.  The file path must be
provided as the next command‑line argument after the option.

.TP
.BR \-c " " \fIsource\fR ", " \-\-code=\fIsource\fR
Interpret the literal Kirby source code supplied on the command line
in
.I source,
again after loading the standard library.

If none of the above options are given, the program prints the usage
message and exits with status 64.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show the help message.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-version
Show the version string.
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-repl
Run the REPL.
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-file
Run a file; the next argument is taken as the file path.
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-code
Run a string of source code; the next argument is interpreted as the
source.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Successful execution or when the user exits the REPL.
.TP
64
Invalid command‑line usage (no valid option supplied).
.TP
65
Compilation error in the executed source.
.TP
70
Runtime error in the executed source.
.TP
Other non‑zero values
Returned by internal calls such as
.BR malloc (3)
or
.BR fopen (3)
 on failure.

